class:: TLogNormalRand
summary:: Triggered lognormal-distribution sampler
categories:: UGens>Generators>Stochastic>Distributions
related:: Guides/Stochastica, Classes/TGaussRand

description::

The Lognormal distribution describes a variable whose logarithm is normally distributed. Mathematically, this produces a strictly positive, right-skewed probability curve. Because human perception of pitch and loudness is inherently logarithmic, this distribution is conceptually ideal for generating random frequencies, random grain sizes in granular synthesis, or amplitude variations, ensuring that the randomized values map cleanly and naturally to human psychoacoustics.

Computes code::exp(mu + abs(sigma)*normal):: using cached Box–Muller normal draws. Exponential overflow returns the largest finite float.

Like link::Classes/TGaussRand::, it is driven by a normal variate; unlike TGaussRand, exponentiation produces positive skewed values and the random sequence is local and resettable.

subsection:: Distribution shape

image::lognormal-density.png#Lognormal densities with mu = 0 and sigma = 0.3, 0.7, and 1, shown in muted teal, blue, and plum. Increasing sigma broadens the positive distribution and lengthens its right tail.::

classmethods::

method:: ar
argument:: mu
Mean of the underlying normal distribution.
argument:: sigma
Underlying normal deviation; magnitude is used.
argument:: trig
Resample on a rising edge.
argument:: seed
Init-rate local seed.
argument:: reset
Restore the seed and sample a new initial value.
argument:: mul
Output multiplier.
argument:: add
Output offset.

method:: kr
Control-rate version with the same arguments.

instancemethods::

examples::

code::
(
{ TLogNormalRand.kr(5.5, 0.5, Impulse.kr(6)).poll }.play
)
::

code::
(
{ SinOsc.ar(TLogNormalRand.kr(6, 0.4, Impulse.kr(8)).clip(80, 4000), 0, 0.08) }.play
)
::

code::
// Psychoacoustic Frequency Generation
(
{
    var trig, freq, sig;
    trig = Impulse.kr(8);
    // Since human pitch perception is logarithmic, a lognormal distribution 
    // creates frequency jumps that sound naturally distributed to our ears.
    freq = TLogNormalRand.kr(mu: 6.0, sigma: 0.5, trig: trig);
    sig = Pulse.ar(freq) * EnvGen.kr(Env.perc(0.01, 0.1), trig);
    Pan2.ar(sig * 0.1)
}.play;
)
::
